    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 3314.8,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 755,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, water is much more than a simple chemical formula. It is the defining substance of our planet and the fundamental prerequisite for life as we know it. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, why it is unique, and why it matters:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical Properties (Why Water is Weird)\nWater is actually a very strange substance compared to most other liquids on Earth. \n\n* **The Three States of Matter:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states: **liquid** (water), **solid** (ice), and **gas** (steam or water vapor).\n* **Ice Floats:** For almost all substances, the solid form is denser than the liquid form (it sinks). Water is an exception. When water freezes, its molecules form a crystalline structure that takes up *more* space, making ice less dense than liquid water. This is why ice cubes float in your drink, and why lakes freeze from the top down, allowing fish to survive the winter underneath.\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water is exceptionally good at dissolving other substances. Because of the electrical polarity of its molecules (the oxygen side has a slight negative charge, and the hydrogen side has a slight positive charge), it easily attracts and breaks down salts, sugars, minerals, and nutrients. This is why rivers and oceans carry so many dissolved minerals (like salt).\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This is why coastal areas have milder climates than inland areas, and it is how the human body regulates its internal temperature through sweat.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\nWater covers roughly **71% of the Earth's surface**, primarily in the oceans. \n* **Oceans:** About **96.5%** of all Earth's water is saltwater in the oceans.\n* **Freshwater:** Only about **2.5%** of Earth's water is fresh. \n* **Availability:** Of that freshwater, over 68% is locked away in glaciers and ice caps, and about 30% is trapped underground. That leaves less than **1%** of Earth's water easily accessible in lakes, rivers, and the atmosphere for human use and ecosystem survival.\n\nWater is constantly moving through the **hydrological cycle** (the water cycle): evaporating from oceans and land, condensing into clouds, falling back to Earth as precipitation (rain or snow), and flowing back to the oceans via rivers.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Water and Life\nBiologically speaking, life cannot exist without water. \n* **The Medium of Life:** The chemical reactions of metabolism and cellular processes inside all living things only happen in aqueous (water-based) environments. \n* **Human Biology:** The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. The brain is about 73% water, the lungs are about 83% water, and even bones are roughly 31% water. We need water to transport nutrients, flush out waste, regulate body temperature, and lubricate joints.\n\n### Summary\nWater is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties. It sculpts the Earth's landscape, drives global weather patterns, regulates climate, and acts as the literal bloodline of every ecosystem on the planet."
